\description{
Fits a bivariate Mixed Poisson (MP) or Zero-Inflated Mixed Poisson
(ZIMP) regression model using inference functions for margins (IFM).

The two marginal models are first fitted separately using \code{\link[=fit_zimp]{fit_zimp()}}.
Dependence between their latent mixing variables is then modeled by a
specified bivariate copula. When both margins are zero-inflated,
dependence between the Bernoulli activation indicators may also be
modeled.
}
\details{
\code{fit_bizimp()} fits one fully specified bivariate model. It does not
perform model selection over marginal distributions or dependence
structures.

Let the two observed counts be

\deqn{Y_j = B_j N_j,\qquad j=1,2,}

where \eqn{N_j} follows a Mixed Poisson model and \eqn{B_j} is an
optional Bernoulli activation indicator. For an MP margin,
\eqn{B_j = 1} identically.

Conditional on the latent intensity,

\deqn{N_j \mid \Lambda_j \sim
      \mathrm{Poisson}(\Lambda_j),}

with marginal mean

\deqn{\mu_{ij} = \exp(x_{ij}^{\top}\beta_j).}

For a zero-inflated margin,

\deqn{\Pr(B_{ij}=1)=\pi_{ij},\qquad
      \mathrm{logit}(\pi_{ij})=z_{ij}^{\top}\gamma_j.}

Thus, \eqn{B_{ij}=1} indicates that observation \eqn{i} belongs to the
count-generating component of margin \eqn{j}.

Dependence between \eqn{\Lambda_1} and \eqn{\Lambda_2} is represented by
the copula specified by \code{mixing.copula}. The copula is evaluated through
a checkerboard approximation with grid size \code{d0}.

When both margins are zero-inflated, dependence between
\eqn{B_1} and \eqn{B_2} is specified separately through
\code{zero.dependence}.
}
\section{Estimation}{


Estimation follows the IFM principle:

\enumerate{
\item Fit margin 1 using \code{\link[=fit_zimp]{fit_zimp()}}.
\item Fit margin 2 independently using \code{\link[=fit_zimp]{fit_zimp()}}.
\item Conditional on the fitted marginal parameters, estimate the
dependence parameter(s) from the bivariate likelihood.
}

Consequently, the marginal estimates returned in \code{fit_margin_1} and
\code{fit_margin_2} are the corresponding independent \code{\link[=fit_zimp]{fit_zimp()}} estimates
and are not re-estimated during dependence fitting.

\code{fit_bizimp()} fits exactly one requested mixing-copula family. When the
requested family is not Gaussian, a Gaussian dependence model may be
fitted internally as a pilot for initialization and dependence-sign
information; it is not an additional candidate model.
}

\section{Marginal configurations}{


Each margin may independently be an MP or ZIMP model. The four supported
configurations are therefore:

\itemize{
\item MP--MP;
\item ZIMP--MP;
\item MP--ZIMP;
\item ZIMP--ZIMP.
}

For direct input, \code{Z = NULL} specifies MP--MP. A list such as
\code{Z = list(Z1, NULL)} specifies ZIMP--MP, while
\code{Z = list(NULL, Z2)} specifies MP--ZIMP. Supplying a shared matrix
\code{Z} applies the zero-component design to both margins.

Dependence between the Bernoulli activation indicators is estimated only
for the ZIMP--ZIMP configuration.
}

\section{Model specification}{


\strong{Formula interface}

Two formulas are supplied, one for each margin. For example,

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{formula1 = y1 ~ 1 + x1 + x2
formula2 = y2 ~ 1 + x1
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

specifies two MP margins. Zero-inflated margins are specified using
two-part formulas:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{formula1 = y1 ~ 1 + x1 + x2 | z1 + z2
formula2 = y2 ~ 1 + x1      | z1
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The expression before \code{|} specifies the count component and the
expression after \code{|} specifies the zero-inflation component.

As in \code{\link[=fit_zimp]{fit_zimp()}}, intercepts are included only when explicitly
requested with \code{1}. This convention applies separately to the count and
zero-inflation components.

\strong{Matrix interface}

\code{Y} may be an \eqn{n \times 2} matrix/data frame or a list containing
two response vectors.

\code{X} may be a common design matrix for both margins or a list
\code{list(X1, X2)} containing margin-specific count designs. Similarly, \code{Z}
may be a common zero-component design or a list containing
margin-specific designs. Intercept columns must be included explicitly
when required.
}

\section{Mixing distributions}{


The mixing distribution is specified separately for each margin through
\code{mixing.distribution.1} and \code{mixing.distribution.2}. Supported
distributions are:

\describe{
\item{\code{"gamma"}}{Gamma mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"lnorm"}}{Lognormal mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"ig"}}{Inverse Gaussian mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"weibull"}}{Weibull mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"loglogistic"}}{Log-logistic mixing distribution.}
\item{\code{"betaprime"}}{Beta-prime mixing distribution.}
}

The two margins may use different mixing distributions.
}

\section{Dependence structure}{


\code{mixing.copula} is a single integer family code supported by
\code{VineCopula}. It specifies the copula connecting the two latent mixing
variables. The default \code{mixing.copula = 1} corresponds to the Gaussian
copula.

For \code{mixing.copula = 2}, corresponding to the Student-t copula, the
degrees of freedom are selected over the values supplied in \code{t.df.grid}.

When both margins are zero-inflated, \code{zero.dependence} specifies the
dependence structure between their Bernoulli activation indicators:

\describe{
\item{\code{"gaussian"}}{
Gaussian copula dependence with an estimated correlation parameter.
}
\item{\code{"comonotone"}}{
Upper Fréchet-bound dependence.
}
\item{\code{"countermonotone"}}{
Lower Fréchet-bound dependence.
}
}
}

\examples{
## Generate a small bivariate Mixed Poisson data set
set.seed(12345)
n <- 50
x <- rnorm(n)
X <- cbind(1, x)

mu1 <- exp(X \%*\% c(0.4, 0.3))
mu2 <- exp(X \%*\% c(0.2, -0.2))

## Gamma-mixed Poisson margins
y1 <- rnbinom(n, size = 1 / 0.2, mu = mu1)
y2 <- rnbinom(n, size = 1 / 0.3, mu = mu2)
Y <- cbind(y1, y2)

## MP--MP model with a Gaussian mixing copula
fit <- fit_bizimp(
  Y = Y,
  X = X,
  mixing.distribution.1 = "gamma",
  mixing.distribution.2 = "gamma",
  var.power = 2,
  mixing.copula = 1L,
  progress = FALSE
)

fit$best_model
fit$AIC
fit$BIC

## Margin-specific designs and a ZIMP--MP configuration
z1 <- rnorm(n)
Z1 <- cbind(z1)

fit_zm <- fit_bizimp(
  Y = Y,
  X = list(X, X),
  Z = list(Z1, NULL),
  mixing.distribution.1 = "gamma",
  mixing.distribution.2 = "gamma",
  var.power = c(2, 2),
  mixing.copula = 1L,
  progress = FALSE
)

fit_zm$zero_inflation

}
